---
// Design review page (261010): ways to present portrait pictures without letting them take too much screen. Not part of the site: remove before launch.
// Real pictures and real wording from the site; texts are shortened with "…" only for this comparison.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import sharp from 'sharp';
import { publicFile } from '../lib/publicfile';

const photo = '/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/mushroom.mind_.jpg';
const caption = 'Illustration from the blog post "The Pedagogy of the Poetic Body"';
const p1 = 'In the learning of a pedagogic practice, there are several phases.';
const p2 = 'The first one is when you learn a particular art from a teacher or a practitioner. In the moment you’re learning that art you’re also learning the way in which this teacher is transmitting the art. There is a direct mimesis between the teacher and the learner: what you learn includes the way you have learnt…';
const p3 = 'The second level of learning happens when as a teacher, you decide to teach that particular art or that particular subject and you learn from yourself what and how you want to teach. You learn from your findings and from your errors. You learn from your intuitions and from your questions…';
const p4 = 'Another level of learning happens if you share the teaching with a colleague and you co-create the work. A two-mind, two-heart, two-body pedagogic sonata. Or a pedagogic chorus if you work in a team.';

const poster = '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/depression_a3_2026.jpg';
const names = ['08','16','20','21','33','62','67','76','89','94','105','111','125','130','138','145','150'];
const gallery = await Promise.all(names.map(async (n) => {
  const src = `/wp-content/uploads/2021/04/181122_clowns_${n}.jpg`;
  const m = await sharp(publicFile(src)!).metadata();
  return { src, w: m.width!, h: m.height!, portrait: m.height! > m.width! };
}));
---
<Base title="Design: portrait pictures" lang="en" path="/design/portrait-images/" section="workshops">
  <h1>Portrait pictures</h1>
  <p class="p2 intro">Review page for the designer, local only. Real pictures from the site. Every treatment keeps the picture whole (nothing is cropped) except where stated. "Landscape box" means a 3:2 rectangle the width of the text column, about 470px tall. Lightboxes always show the full picture, so they are not shown here.</p>

  <h2 class="h3 sec">A. Pictures inside page text</h2>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">0 · Today</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">The portrait runs the full width of the text, so it is taller than a laptop screen.</p>
    <div class="col"><p>{p1}</p><p>{p4}</p>
      <figure class="t0"><img src={photo} alt="" /><figcaption class="p3">{caption}</figcaption></figure>
      <p>{p3}</p></div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">1 · Shrink to the landscape box, centred (your original rule)</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">The picture is scaled down so it fits inside the box and centred; white space on both sides. Simple; the caption is centred under it.</p>
    <div class="col"><p>{p1}</p><p>{p4}</p>
      <figure class="t1"><img src={photo} alt="" /><figcaption class="p3">{caption}</figcaption></figure>
      <p>{p3}</p></div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">2 · Landscape frame, light grey ground (recommended)</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">The portrait sits whole, centred, in a 3:2 frame on light grey, the same treatment as the workshop slideshow. The caption lines up with the frame and the page rhythm stays regular.</p>
    <div class="col"><p>{p1}</p><p>{p4}</p>
      <figure class="t2"><div class="frame"><img src={photo} alt="" /></div><figcaption class="p3">{caption}</figcaption></figure>
      <p>{p3}</p></div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">3 · Landscape frame, blurred copy of the picture behind it</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">As option 2, but the empty sides are filled with a soft blurred, enlarged copy of the same picture, so there are no flat grey bars. More polished; needs a little extra markup that the build adds automatically.</p>
    <div class="col"><p>{p1}</p><p>{p4}</p>
      <figure class="t3"><div class="frame"><img class="bg" src={photo} alt="" /><img class="fg" src={photo} alt="" /></div><figcaption class="p3">{caption}</figcaption></figure>
      <p>{p3}</p></div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">4 · Picture beside the text</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">The portrait floats to the right at about 40% of the width and the text wraps beside it. It uses the empty space instead of shrinking the picture. Works best on long text (About, blog posts); on a short paragraph it leaves a gap.</p>
    <div class="col t4"><figure><img src={photo} alt="" /><figcaption class="p3">{caption}</figcaption></figure><p>{p1}</p><p>{p2}</p><p>{p3}</p><p>{p4}</p></div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">5 · Height cap only</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">The picture is only limited to 70% of the window height. Gentle, but it is still a tall picture on a short screen.</p>
    <div class="col"><p>{p1}</p><p>{p4}</p>
      <figure class="t5"><img src={photo} alt="" /><figcaption class="p3">{caption}</figcaption></figure>
      <p>{p3}</p></div></section>

  <h2 class="h3 sec">B. Event posters (text printed on the picture)</h2>
  <p class="p2 intro">A poster carries the dates, venue and booking details, so shrinking it to a landscape box makes that text unreadable.</p>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">B1 · Poster beside the details (recommended)</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">The poster takes about 40% of the width on the left at its natural proportions, with the title, date and place on the right, as on the Events list. It stays readable and no space is wasted.</p>
    <div class="col wide b1"><figure><img src={poster} alt="" /></figure>
      <div><h3 class="et">THE DEPRESSED CLOWN</h3><p class="tight"><strong>May 14 - 16, 2026</strong></p><p class="tight">Padua, Italy</p><p class="mt">Then the Description, Location, Admission and Tickets sections follow below as today.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">B2 · Small poster with a "View full poster" link</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">The poster is shown in the landscape frame at a size that shows the design but not the small print; selecting it, or the link, opens it large in the viewer.</p>
    <div class="col"><h3 class="et">THE DEPRESSED CLOWN</h3><p class="tight"><strong>May 14 - 16, 2026</strong></p><p>Padua, Italy</p>
      <figure class="t2"><div class="frame"><img src={poster} alt="" /></div><figcaption class="p3"><a href={poster}>View full poster ↗</a></figcaption></figure></div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">B3 · Exempt: posters stay full size</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">Today's look. A per-picture setting ("show full size") keeps posters out of the rule.</p></section>

  <h2 class="h3 sec">C. Photo gallery pages (Melbourne Red Nose Intensive, 17 photos shown)</h2>
  <p class="p2 intro">Today every thumbnail is a 4:3 crop, so portrait photos lose the top and bottom (heads are cut).</p>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">C0 · Today: 4:3 crop</h3>
    <div class="g0">{gallery.map((g) => <img src={g.src} alt="" />)}</div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">C1 · Same grid, whole picture on a grey tile</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">Every tile stays 4:3, but portrait photos are shown whole with grey on either side. Tidy and regular, but the grey bars are noticeable.</p>
    <div class="g1">{gallery.map((g) => <span><img src={g.src} alt="" /></span>)}</div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">C2 · Masonry columns (recommended)</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">Photos keep their real proportions and flow into columns. No cropping and no bars; this is how most photo galleries work. The reading order runs down each column.</p>
    <div class="g2">{gallery.map((g) => <img src={g.src} alt="" />)}</div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">C3 · Mixed grid: portrait photos get tall tiles</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">Landscape photos take one tile; portrait photos take two rows, so nothing is cropped heavily and the grid stays orderly. Slightly irregular at the end.</p>
    <div class="g3">{gallery.map((g) => <img class:list={[g.portrait && 'tall']} src={g.src} alt="" />)}</div></section>

  <section class="opt"><h3 class="oh">C4 · Same crop, anchored on the top (faces)</h3>
    <p class="p2 why">The smallest change: keep the 4:3 crop but cut from the bottom, not the middle, so faces stay in. Still a crop.</p>
    <div class="g0 top">{gallery.map((g) => <img src={g.src} alt="" />)}</div></section>

  <section class="opt rec"><h3 class="oh">My recommendation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Pictures in text: <strong>option 2</strong> (landscape frame on light grey), matching the slideshow. Option 3 if you want it softer.</li>
      <li>Event posters: <strong>B1</strong>, the poster beside the details.</li>
      <li>Phones: leave portrait pictures at full width, where a portrait fills the screen naturally. The rule applies from tablet width (768px) up.</li>
      <li>Gallery pages: <strong>C2</strong> masonry (or C3 if you prefer a strict grid).</li>
      <li>Open any content picture in the lightbox when selected, so small pictures can still be seen large.</li>
      <li>One definition of "portrait" everywhere: width less than 1.2 times the height (this includes near-square pictures; the slideshow already uses it).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro { max-width: 44rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .sec { margin: 4rem 0 .5rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 3px solid var(--text); }
  .opt { margin: 2.5rem 0; }
  .oh { font-size: var(--h3-size); margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
  .why { max-width: 44rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .rec { border-top: 3px solid var(--red-text); padding-top: 1rem; }
  .col { max-width: 44rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem 2rem; }
  .col.wide { max-width: 52rem; }
  .col p { margin: 0 0 var(--space-p); }
  .col p.tight { margin: 0; }
  .col p.mt { margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .col figure { margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .col figcaption { margin-top: .5rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .et { font-size: var(--h3-size); margin: 0 0 .5rem; }

  .t0 img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .t1 { text-align: center; }
  .t1 img { display: block; margin: 0 auto; width: auto; height: auto; max-width: 100%; max-height: calc((44rem - 4rem) * 2 / 3); }
  .t2 .frame, .t3 .frame { aspect-ratio: 3 / 2; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; overflow: hidden; position: relative; }
  .t2 .frame { background: #f3f3f3; }
  .t2 img { display: block; max-width: 100%; max-height: 100%; width: auto; height: auto; }
  .t2 figcaption, .t3 figcaption { text-align: center; }
  .t3 .frame { background: #ddd; }
  .t3 .bg { position: absolute; inset: -10%; width: 120%; height: 120%; object-fit: cover; filter: blur(22px) brightness(.92); }
  .t3 .fg { position: relative; display: block; max-width: 100%; max-height: 100%; width: auto; height: auto; }
  .t4 figure { float: right; width: 40%; margin: .25rem 0 1rem 1.5rem; }
  .t4 img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .t5 img { display: block; margin: 0 auto; width: auto; height: auto; max-width: 100%; max-height: 70vh; }
  .t5 figcaption { text-align: center; }

  .b1 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 40% 1fr; gap: 2rem; align-items: start; }
  .b1 figure { margin: 0; }
  .b1 img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }

  .g0, .g1, .g2, .g3 { max-width: 64rem; }
  .g0 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 4px; }
  .g0 img { width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 4 / 3; object-fit: cover; display: block; }
  .g0.top img { object-position: top; }
  .g1 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 4px; }
  .g1 span { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; background: #f3f3f3; aspect-ratio: 4 / 3; }
  .g1 img { max-width: 100%; max-height: 100%; width: auto; height: auto; display: block; }
  .g2 { columns: 4; column-gap: 4px; }
  .g2 img { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin: 0 0 4px; break-inside: avoid; }
  .g3 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); grid-auto-rows: 9rem; grid-auto-flow: dense; gap: 4px; }
  .g3 img { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; display: block; }
  .g3 img.tall { grid-row: span 2; }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) { .g0, .g1, .g3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } .g2 { columns: 2; } .b1 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .t4 figure { float: none; width: 100%; margin: 1rem 0; } }
</style>
